```rust
fn load_config(program_id: &Pubkey, account: &AccountInfo) -> Result<BridgeConfig, ProgramError> {
    if account.key != &config_pda(program_id).0 {
        return Err(BridgeError::WrongPda.into());
    }
    let config = BridgeConfig::try_from_slice(&account.data.borrow())?;
    if !config.is_initialized {
        return Err(BridgeError::NotInitialized.into());
    }
    Ok(config)
}
```

It never verifies that `account.owner == program_id`. An attacker can pre‑create the PDA with the system program (or any other owner) and populate it with a forged `BridgeConfig` (e.g., setting themselves as `admin`, pausing the bridge, etc.). Subsequent calls that rely on `load_config` will accept the malicious state, leading to a full takeover of the bridge logic. The missing owner check is the root cause.

